<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for macrdpController.app when it embeds + activates the macrdp
  Camera system extension (camera redirection Phase 3).

  Only used when gui/make-tray-app.sh is run with CAMERA_EXTENSION=1 (the
  camera-extension build); the normal controller build signs with NO entitlements
  and does NOT embed the extension (unchanged, byte-identical).

  ONE entitlement, and deliberately so:
  * com.apple.developer.system-extension.install — lets this app request activation
    of a system extension it embeds (OSSystemExtensionRequest). SELF-SERVICEABLE in
    the Apple Developer portal (add the "System Extension" capability to the App ID;
    no Apple approval request, unlike DriverKit / the USB host-controller path).

  The controller is deliberately NOT sandboxed and does NOT list the App Group:
    - macrdpController drives `launchctl`, edits ~/Library, opens files — app-sandbox
      would break all of it. An unsandboxed Developer-ID app CAN hold
      system-extension.install and activate a CMIO extension (OBS does exactly this).
    - The App Group belongs only on the EXTENSION (it names the extension's CMIO
      Mach service). The controller merely activates; the decoded-frame feed to the
      extension comes from macrdp.app via the CoreMediaIO client API, not the group.

  This set MUST be a subset of the controller's provisioning profile's entitlements
  or codesign fails. Because system-extension.install is a restricted entitlement,
  the controller app also embeds its provisioning profile (make-tray-app.sh).
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.developer.system-extension.install</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
